.TH "RCSH" 1R
.UC 4
.SH NAME
rcsh \- remote access version
.SH SYNOPSIS
Same as
.I csh
but with remote access.
.SH DESCRIPTION
Same as described in UNIX Programmer's Manual (1), except that
remote file name
.I [host:]path
can be used in name substitution and I/O
redirection.
.PP
.B Name substitution (globbing):
the characters `*', `?', `[' and `{' in
.I path
are expanded on the machine
.I host
if
.I host:
is present or on the local machine if
.I host:
is omitted.
However, `~' can be expanded only if
.I host:
is missing (i.e. as the first character of the whole file name).
No substitution is allowed in the
.I host
part.
.PP
.B I/O redirection:
standard input, standard output and diagnostic output can be redirected
to remote files in the same way as to local files.
.PP
The other functions such as command interpretation, history substitution,
alias substitution and variable substitution are the same as described
in UNIX Programmer's Manual (1).
.SH RESTRICTIONS
.I Rcsh
supports remote file access but not remote command execution.
The commands are always executed locally.  The default input/output is
the local terminal.  For example, the following commands are legal:
.PP
.DT
	cat mordred:... arthur:... | pg
.PP
.DT
	ls -l mordred:... | grep ... > arthur:...
.PP
But
.PP
.DT
	ls ... | mordred:grep ...
.PP
does not work.
.PP
There is no remote current working directory.  Therefore,
.PP
.DT
	cd
.I host:path ,
or
.PP
.DT
	pushd
.I host:path
.PP
does not work.
.SH BUGS
The built-in commands
do not accept remote files as arguments or input/output redirection.
For example,
.PP
.DT
	echo "..." >mordred:... , or
.PP
.DT
	exec ls arthur:...
.PP
does not work.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
commands(1R), libra.a(3R)
.PP
csh(1), rsh(1)
.PP
Walter F. Tichy and Zuwang Ruan,
.I "Towards a Distributed File System,"
Proceedings of USENIX 1984 Summer Conference.
